\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Alex has become a caricature because he no longer just tests claims. He turns them into elaborate philosophical exercises loaded with fallacies and logical errors.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Instead of identifying the load bearing premises, attacking the weak points, and asking whether the argument survives, he invites you to smell the shit from every angle and reconsider whether it might somehow be something else.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He has drifted away from better epistemology and toward unnecessary complexity dressed up as intellectual sophistication.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The format rewards it, more ambiguity, more pondering, more engagement. Longer videos.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Meanwhile, the basic logical failures get buried under layers of nuance.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Sometimes a bad argument only needs one thing: find the unsupported premise, expose the fallacy, and knock the argument down.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Which is why it was refreshing when one of his guests finally called him on it and walked out.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Hitchens. That interview nails my point.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Alex finds a decent epistemic point, then buries it under endless circling, hypotheticals, and recycled questions. Hitchens finally calls him out for going in circles and not listening.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 That is Alex now. Every weak claim gets dragged through a two hour philosophical maze.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 No. Find the premise. Test the evidence. Expose the failure. Move on.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Alex literally says the plan was an hour on drugs and another hour on god.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 That is the caricature. He is full of shit.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Good thing I explore these videos with ad blockers and work from the transcripts. These motherfuckers do not deserve hours of my time just because they figured out how to monetize intellectual masturbation.}
