\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 Just so we're clear, the Navier Stokes equation already fundamentally doesn't capture the physics of real liquids. As a model of incompressible fluids, Navier Stokes equation cannot even describe sound waves traveling through water.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 It is a continuum-level model for substances that in reality have atomistic and molecular nature. For studying fluids in many contexts, that assumption is okay (ie for problems that have phenomenology that proceed at a length scale greater than atomistic and molecular scale).}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 My understanding of the clay millennium math problem is that: we already know that Navier Stokes is unphysical, but is it }{\f0\fs24\b0\i1 devastatingly}{\f0\fs24\b0  unphysical? Does it have energy divergences where there shouldn't be?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Having spoken to a lot of mathematicians I think that none of them really care about the physics consequence. It's all a kind of analytical exercise in very complicated PDEs. I think what the mathematicians are doing Feynman would call masturbatory. (I'm only saying this because this is the physics subreddit).}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 From a physics perspective I think it is a very interesting question to next ask, if indeed we have proven that the Navier Stokes equation has this devastating unphysicality to it, how can we modify the representation to be more in line with real substances? And in what physical situations would that matter?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 It reminds me a lot of the ultraviolet catastrophe, where Maxwell's equations were successful in a lot of cases in electromagnetism, but was failing for blackbody radiation. And it was only by including and rationalizing the quantized nature of photons that Einstein was able to resolve it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 A finite time blow up in the Navier Stokes equation is also a 'catastrophe' in fluid dynamics. Maybe the energy divergence can be resolved by quantizing the energy of rotational vortices, or some other analogy to Einstein's solution to blackbody radiation.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 But again, another problem is to ask when does this matter, and in what interesting context would it make new predictions? For Einstein it was for the photoelectric effect. What is the analogous photoelectric effect for fluid flow?}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 Yeah, I dunno. Honestly I think the comparison was kind of a dumb one in the first place, and I find the sudden switch from the ultraviolet catastrophe and blackbody spectrum to the photoelectric effect, as if they're the same thing, really odd. If I heard a colleague say something like that, it'd raise eyebrows. It's something I'd expect more from a misinformed physics enthusiast than a physicist, and frankly, the unnecessary Feynman comment only adds to that perception. But it's possible it was just a massive brain fart. It does happen from time to time.}{ }
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 No, the original commenter is simply wrong. The Navier Stokes equations are compressible by nature. They would only not be if you applied an assumption of constant density. "Compressible Navier Stokes" is redundant. However, the Millennium Prize only dealt with the incompressible version.}{ }
\par\plain 
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 NS is not a model of incompressible fluids, and sounds waves can be described by it. As a matter of fact sound is a perturbation of pressure and density and sound waves arise naturally from solving the full compressible NS equations. For example, if you have accurate enough CFD simulation (both in mesh quality, domain size, boundary conditions,etc) physical sounds wave will be observable. Moreover linear acoustic equation for simple case can be solved by hand, if you want to try it out. What you might be thinking of are "pointwise" discontinuities e.g. shocks and expansions fans that are not allowed in the NS weak form and that are non-physical, as the real phenomenon happens at a scale such that the continuum hypothesis and local equilibrium hypotesis don't hold true anymore. I really don't understand what's going on with all the replies agreeing with your reply, in a physics subreddit to boot.}{ }
\par\plain 
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 We don't universally kill peasants on demand for organ transplants for the rich. We don't have unrestricted slavery in Western Society. And so on. The truth is morality wins quite a lot. I view it as a real quantifying force in the equation of the universe whether you agree or not doesn't really matter; many do and it produces real changes to their actions and as a cumulative sum to society.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 The point is beliefs or ideals are frequently being balanced against profit and profit almost never is completely* optimized at the expense of others. It is a very privileged and regurgitated easy platitude to pretend that idealism doesn't ever lead to changes when almost every positively moral change has been due to idealists. And it is trivially easy to prove so if you simply look around you and do any light reading with an already not made up mind.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 And I can go on and on. But in this specific case, the large dissonance between "Terence Tao is hoping to contribute to the discussion and ethics of how to treat AI going forward" and "Terence Tao is trying to stop AI from existing" is the main contributor to the average layperson misunderstanding.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Mathematics is in danger of a major brain drain in the long term, maybe even very short term, where people with true passion may become disillusioned and insular, and students begin to avoid the subject entirely. Why work on something for years when AI can solve it in days. People effectively now are recognizing that they are in competition instead of collaboration. Siloed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 The thing is though, what happens if and when we find AI doesn't fulfill all its promises. By then the pursuit may have shifted fundamentally, like nuclear moving effectively from physics to politics, and becoming majorly restricted. Mathematics is a human construct, and AI may well have areas in which it doesn't excel or even approach human thought imo. If we can't work together with it, we may end up in a situation like nuclear is today, which is effectively now niche and miles away, light years, from what its early potential was. Stalemate.}
